      ":question/problem": "Consider the following initial value problem.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\left(1\\right)y=6,\\quad y(0)=-2\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\nAn integrating factor for this equation is $I(x)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\nThe solution to the initial value problem is $y(x)=$ {{field-2}}.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x),\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=1$ and $Q(x)=6.$\n\nWe compute an integrating factor using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & =e^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =e^{\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =e^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation in standard form by $I(x)=e^{x}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e^{x}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+1y) & =e^{x}\\cdot 6 \\\\ e^{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+e^{x}y & =6 e^{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ye^{x}) & =6 e^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ye^{x})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 6 e^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ ye^{x} & =6\\left(1 e^{x}\\right)+C \\\\ ye^{x} & =6 e^{x}+C \\\\ y & =\\left(C + 6 e^{x}\\right) e^{- x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the general solution. To find the value of $C$ corresponding to the specific solution, we apply the initial condition $y(0)=-2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =\\frac{(6 e^{(0)})+C}{(e^{(0)})} \\\\ -2 & =\\frac{6+C}{1} \\\\ C & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=6 - 8 e^{- x}.\n$$",
